{"chunk_id": "f57e85b0dbd9", "text": "Vahuka (the disguised Nala) witnesses King Rituparna\u2019s astonishing ability to instantly calculate the number of leaves and fruits on a Vibhitaka tree. In exchange for revealing this numerical and dice knowledge, Nala offers his unmatched mastery of horses. When Nala learns the science of dice, the malevolent spirit Kali\u2014who had possessed him since his gambling downfall\u2014can no longer remain within him and is forced out, weakened by Damayanti\u2019s curse and the serpent Karkotaka\u2019s poison. Kali begs forgiveness, promising that those who recite Nala\u2019s story will be safe from him. Freed from this influence, Nala drives Rituparna swiftly to Vidarbha. Meanwhile, Damayanti hears the unmistakable thunder of Nala\u2019s chariot and hopes desperately that her lost husband has finally returned.\nNala learns dice from Rituparna, expels Kali from his body, and arrives in Vidarbha disguised while Damayanti senses his presence by the sound of the chariot.\nThemes: liberation from curse, exchange of knowledge, recognition through signs, love and longing, fate and redemption", "vector": [0.006446646060794592, -0.018165001645684242, 0.023650335147976875, -0.016566261649131775, -0.016580043360590935, 0.0168143417686224, -0.03757040575146675, -0.01747588999569416, -0.03153378143906593, -0.01935027539730072, 0.01755858212709427, 0.008338259533047676, 0.010474507696926594, 0.01227998174726963, 0.011825167573988438, 0.0143473194912076, 0.03657808527350426, -0.02276827208697796, 0.021334918215870857, -0.022203199565410614, -0.0024704677052795887, 0.011287660337984562, 0.014264625497162342, 0.006753300782293081, -0.028501687571406364, -0.0006852360675111413, 0.01529829390347004, -0.011136055923998356, 0.010846628807485104, -0.014223278500139713, 0.00045782900997437537, -0.0021913773380219936, 0.0049547175876796246, -0.01943296752870083, -0.027454236522316933, -0.005195907317101955, 0.005623156670480967, -0.009447730146348476, 0